WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.842
ANY and ALL can be summarized with a simple contrast.

00:00:03.842 --> 00:00:07.222
ANY needs at least one successful comparison.

00:00:07.222 --> 00:00:10.317
ALL needs every comparison to succeed.

00:00:10.317 --> 00:00:13.597
Equality with ANY behaves much like IN.

00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:21.437
These are useful intuitions, but always remember that NULL values and empty sets can affect the formal SQL

00:00:21.437 --> 00:00:24.957
logic. ANY and ALL compare one value with a set.

00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:29.737
ANY needs the comparison to succeed against at least one returned value;

00:00:29.737 --> 00:00:33.822
ALL requires it to succeed against every returned value.

00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:40.732
Empty sets and NULLs can affect the result, so test those cases explicitly when the set is data-driven.
